<Base title="Senj"
  eyebrow="The town the railway went around"
  dek="Senj made this family and then stopped being able to feed it. Every occupation in the record — day labourer, porter, sailor, cigarette-factory hand — is a consequence of one decision taken in 1873.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Senj sits under the Velebit at the head of the <em>Senjska draga</em>, where the
    <strong>bura</strong> comes off the mountain hard enough to stop traffic. It is one of the
    oldest towns on the Croatian coast, and for four hundred years it was a place of consequence:
    a free royal town, a bishopric, a Habsburg military port, and the home of the
    <strong>Uskoks</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1537–1618</div>
    <h2>The Uskoks and Nehaj</h2>
    <p>When the Ottomans took Klis in 1537, its defenders — refugees the Slavs called
    <em>uskoci</em>, “the ones who jumped in” — fell back to Senj. The Habsburgs put them on the
    frontier payroll and let them raid. They became, depending on who was writing, a Christian
    bulwark or a pirate confederacy; in practice both, because Vienna paid them irregularly and
    they took the difference out of Venetian and Ottoman shipping.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">In <strong>1558</strong> the captain
    <strong>Ivan Lenković</strong> built <strong>Nehaj</strong> on the hill above the town, using
    stone from the churches and monasteries outside the walls so that a besieger would have nothing
    to shelter behind. It was the Uskok headquarters until the <strong>Uskok War of
    1615–18</strong> ended with Venice and Vienna agreeing to disperse them inland. Nehaj still
    stands over the town, and it is the thing everyone photographs.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>This archive claims no Uskok descent.</strong> The Uskoks were broken up in 1618 and
      the earliest person in this tree is born about <strong>1717</strong> — a century later. What
      the Uskok period explains is not the family's blood but the family's
      <em>place</em>: it is why there was a fortified, garrisoned, Habsburg-administered town here
      at all, and why the empty mountain behind it was about to be settled on purpose.
      <a href={u("/bunjevci")}>The settlement →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The turning point</div>
    <h2>1873: the railway goes to Rijeka</h2>
    <p class="lede">Senj's whole nineteenth century was built on being the port where the Lika
    highlands met the sea. Timber, wool and livestock came down the roads from the interior and went
    out through Senj. Then the <strong>Karlovac–Rijeka railway opened in 1873 and did not come
    here.</strong></p>
    <p>The traffic followed the rails to Rijeka. Senj's port trade collapsed, and it never
    recovered. This is the single most important economic fact in this archive, because the family's
    recorded work sits directly downstream of it:</p>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <tr><th><a href={u("/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic")}>Toma Blažević</a><br /><span class="dim">1838–1907</span></th>
        <td><strong><em>Nadničar</em></strong> — day labourer. Born at
        <em>Br. 114, Smokvica Krmpotska</em>; died at Senj and buried there. A man who came off the
        mountain to a town that had no work left.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><a href={u("/people/josip-anton-papic")}>Josip “Joso” Papić</a><br /><span class="dim">1857–1939</span></th>
        <td><strong><em>Trhonoša, bremenar, gostioničar</em></strong> — load-carrier, porter,
        innkeeper. The first two are the same job twice: carrying other people's goods on your back
        for a fee. The third is what he made of it.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><a href={u("/people/ljubomir-blazevic")}>Ljubomir Blažević</a><br /><span class="dim">1892–1966</span></th>
        <td><strong><em>Mornar</em></strong> — sailor. Born at <strong>Rijeka</strong>, not Senj —
        the family had already followed the work to the port that got the railway. He came back, and
        died at Senj.</td></tr>
      <tr><th><a href={u("/people/milka-lucia-papic")}>Milka Papić</a><br /><span class="dim">1886–1971</span></th>
        <td><strong>Supervisor of workers at the cigarette factory</strong>,
        <em>Tvornica duhana</em>, Senj. Her sister-in-law <strong>Anka Blažević</strong>
        (1893–1953) was a <em>radnica</em> — a floor worker — at the same factory.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The tobacco factory</h2>
    <p>The Habsburg tobacco monopoly was extended to Croatia, Slavonia and the Military Frontier by
    an imperial patent of <strong>20 November 1850</strong>, effective from March 1851, and state
    tobacco factories followed across the region. Senj's was one of them, and for two generations of
    this family it was the employer of last resort in a town with no port trade — women's work,
    indoors, waged, and steady in a way that carrying sacks was not.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">After the Second World War the Senj factory was
    <strong>liquidated</strong> and its cigarette-making machinery was transferred to the tobacco
    factory at <strong>Rovinj</strong>. The tree records the building's later life exactly: the
    address is given as <em class="mono">Ul. Stjepana Radića 4, 53270 Senj</em>, with the note that
    it “now is a textile factory”.</p>
    <div class="note" style="border-left-color:var(--r-modern)"><strong>And it is why these
    families met.</strong> One page of the Senj marriage register for 1918 carries three brides
    entered as <em>radnica u tvornici duhana</em> — from <strong>Otočac/Šumećica</strong>, from
    <strong>Krivi Put</strong>, and from <strong>Senj</strong>. Those are the three places this
    archive comes from. Counting <a href={u("/milka")}>Milka</a>, her sister-in-law
    <strong>Anka</strong>, <strong>Antonija Blažević</strong> and <strong>Ana Papić</strong>, six
    women of these families worked that floor. The factory was <strong>the institution that brought
    the mountain villages into the town</strong> — and it is a large part of why a Blažević of
    Smokvica, a Prpić of Krivi Put and a Žubrinić of Otočac end up in one family.</div>

    <div class="note"><strong>Unverified here.</strong> The Senj factory's founding date, size and
    closure year have not been confirmed from a Croatian source by this archive. The monopoly patent
    and the transfer of machinery to Rovinj are documented generally; the specific history of the
    Senj plant is <a href={u("/open-questions")}>an open question</a>.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Two days that finished what the railway started</div>
    <h2>7–8 October 1943</h2>
    <p class="lede">After Italy's capitulation Senj became one of the most important
    <strong>Partisan ports</strong> on the Adriatic, and German aircraft bombed the town and harbour
    over two days in October 1943. <strong>Thirty local residents were killed</strong> and about
    <strong>half the town was destroyed to its foundations</strong>; only a quarter of the buildings
    were still habitable.</p>
    <p>Hedviga was seventeen. Her aunt's husband died on the second day.
    <a href={u("/senj-1943")}>Senj, 1943 →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And then the boats out</h2>
    <p>A port town with no port sends its people away. This tree records the destinations plainly:
    <a href={u("/port-pirie")}><strong>Port Pirie</strong> in South Australia</a>, where
    <strong>Ivan Žubrinić</strong> went about 1884 and where his descendants still are;
    <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>, where Hedviga's cousin <strong>Oskar Kosina</strong> died in
    1954; and then, two generations later, <strong>Africa</strong> — Hedviga to Rhodesia and then to
    <a href={u("/hedviga")}>Johannesburg in April 1975</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
